{UTF-8}
********************************************************
	.Neighbourhoods
********************************************************~
		One model for every city. Cities differ only in their parameter values; the
		structure is the same everywhere. Within a city, two neighbourhood types
		capture the difference that matters most for justice.
	|

Neighbourhood:
	Deprived, Other
	~
	~	Two neighbourhood types within one city. Deprived: the most socioeconomically \
		deprived areas, for example the most deprived quintile of small areas. Other: \
		the rest of the city. Every city uses the same two elements; what differs \
		between cities is the parameter values attached to them.
	|

City:
	Generic, Cork, Klagenfurt, Lahti, Malta
	~
	~	One structure, five cities; they do not interact. Generic is the \
		illustrative mid-size city of v4 to v5.1 and reproduces v5.1 exactly. The \
		four pilots of the proposal: Cork (Ireland), Klagenfurt (Austria), Lahti \
		(the Paijat-Hame pilot), Malta (the national conurbation). Only the \
		parameters in the City Profile groups differ between cities; every other \
		parameter is shared. New in v6.
	|

********************************************************
	.Stocks
********************************************************~
		Four accumulations. Functioning NBS is kept separately for each
		neighbourhood type, in hectares of green cover. Capability and support are
		citywide 0-1 indices, as in v3. Residents displaced is the one new stock.
	|

Functioning NBS[City, Neighbourhood]=
	INTEG (
	NBS Implementation[City, Neighbourhood]-Loss of Functionality[City, Neighbourhood],
		MIN ( Initial NBS Cover[City, Neighbourhood] * Land Area[City, Neighbourhood],
		 Maximum Feasible NBS[City, Neighbourhood] ) )
	~	ha [0,?]
	~	STOCK 1. Green infrastructure delivered and kept up by the city's NBS \
		programme that is actually functioning: street trees, parks, green roofs, \
		rain gardens, bioswales, wetlands. Measured as hectares of green cover, the \
		quantity satellite land-cover data such as Copernicus report, so that every \
		city can be initialised from the same source. Installed NBS is not the same \
		as functioning NBS.
	|

Municipal NBS Capability[City]=
	INTEG (
	Capability Building[City]-Capability Erosion[City],
		Initial Municipal NBS Capability)
	~	Dmnl [0,1]
	~	STOCK 2. Organisational ability to plan, procure, implement, coordinate, \
		monitor and maintain NBS, as a fraction of best practice. Unchanged from v3.
	|

Public and Political Support for NBS[City]=
	INTEG (
	Support Building[City]-Support Erosion[City],
		Initial Public and Political Support)
	~	Dmnl [0,1]
	~	STOCK 3. Societal and political support, 0 to 1. Unchanged from v3.
	|

Residents Displaced by Green Gentrification[City]=
	INTEG (
	Green Gentrification Displacement[City],
		0)
	~	person [0,?]
	~	STOCK 4. Cumulative number of original residents of deprived neighbourhoods \
		pushed out because greening raised rents and property values. The concrete \
		unintended consequence of targeting NBS at the areas that need it most.
	|

********************************************************
	.Flows
********************************************************~
		Inflows and outflows of the four stocks.
	|

NBS Implementation[City, Neighbourhood]=
	Remaining Feasible NBS Opportunity[City, Neighbourhood] * MIN (
		 Resources for NBS Delivery[City] * Share of Resources to Rollout[City] * Share of New NBS[Neighbourhood]
		 / Delivery Cost per Hectare[City] * NBS Implementation and Design Quality[City],
		 Maximum Feasible NBS[City, Neighbourhood] / Minimum Delivery Time )
	~	ha/Year
	~	Budget buys hectares of functioning green cover. The rollout budget is split \
		between neighbourhoods by the targeting lever and discounted by delivery \
		quality, which is what capability buys. Two limits then apply: the room \
		left in each neighbourhood, and how fast that room can physically be \
		filled. The second never binds in the default city; it stops a small \
		neighbourhood with a large budget being filled faster than projects can \
		be delivered.
	|

Loss of Functionality[City, Neighbourhood]=
	Functioning NBS[City, Neighbourhood] / Base NBS Lifetime * Decay Multiplier[City]
	~	ha/Year
	~	Degradation of the installed base, as in v3. Assets wear out over their \
		base lifetime, faster when delivery quality is poor.
	|

Capability Building[City]=
	( 1 - Municipal NBS Capability[City] ) * MIN ( Maximum Improvement Absorption,
		 XIDZ ( Capability Improvement Effort[City], Reference Capability Investment[City], 0 ) )
		 / Capability Build Time
	~	Dmnl/Year
	~	v6: XIDZ, because a city with no NBS budget now has no reference investment \
		either, and 0 divided by 0 is defined here as no capability building. \
		Learning, training, coordination routines and process improvement. \
		Unchanged from v3.
	|

Capability Erosion[City]=
	Municipal NBS Capability[City] * Staff and Knowledge Loss
	~	Dmnl/Year
	~	Decay of skills and routines with staff turnover. Unchanged from v3.
	|

Support Building[City]=
	( 1 - Public and Political Support for NBS[City] ) * Realized NBS Performance[City]
		 / Support Adjustment Time
	~	Dmnl/Year
	~	Support builds toward what delivered performance warrants. Unchanged from v3.
	|

Support Erosion[City]=
	Public and Political Support for NBS[City] * ( Baseline Support Decay
		 + Harm Sensitivity * Unintended Social Ecological and Economic Consequences[City] )
		 / Support Adjustment Time
	~	Dmnl/Year
	~	Erosion from salient adverse effects, now including displacement, plus a \
		baseline fade. Unchanged in form from v3.
	|

Green Gentrification Displacement[City]=
	( Population[City, Deprived] - Residents Displaced by Green Gentrification[City] )
		 * Displacement Sensitivity to Greening
		 * NBS Implementation[City, Deprived] / Land Area[City, Deprived]
		 * ( 1 - Effective Displacement Safeguards )
	~	person/Year
	~	Original residents of deprived neighbourhoods displaced each year. \
		Proportional to how many are still there, to how fast their neighbourhood \
		is being greened (share of its land greened per year), and to the gap left \
		by anti-displacement safeguards. Greening elsewhere in the city does not \
		displace anyone here.
	|

********************************************************
	.Delivery Engine
********************************************************~
		The v3 structure: capability sets quality, quality sets decay and harm,
		the performance shortfall drives the split between rollout and capability.
	|

NBS Implementation and Design Quality[City]=
	Minimum Quality + ( 1 - Minimum Quality ) * Municipal NBS Capability[City]
	~	Dmnl
	~	Quality embodied in what gets built and how well it is kept up, 0 to 1. \
		Capability is its sole determinant, as in v3.
	|

Decay Multiplier[City]=
	1 + Extra Decay at Zero Quality * ( 1 - NBS Implementation and Design Quality[City] )
	~	Dmnl
	~	How much faster the installed base wears out than at perfect delivery \
		quality. Unchanged from v3.
	|

Total Functioning NBS[City]=
	SUM ( Functioning NBS[City, Neighbourhood!] )
	~	ha
	~	Functioning NBS across the whole city.
	|

Maximum Feasible NBS[City, Neighbourhood]=
	MIN ( Maximum NBS Cover[Neighbourhood], 1 - Background Green Cover[City, Neighbourhood] )
		 * Land Area[City, Neighbourhood]
	~	ha
	~	Ceiling on programme NBS in each neighbourhood. The MIN keeps total green \
		cover at or below 100 percent whatever values a city enters.
	|

Remaining Feasible NBS Opportunity[City, Neighbourhood]=
	MAX ( 0, 1 - XIDZ ( Functioning NBS[City, Neighbourhood], Maximum Feasible NBS[City, Neighbourhood], 1 ) )
	~	Dmnl
	~	Fraction of each neighbourhood's feasible room not yet used. Produces \
		saturation.
	|

Benefit per Hectare of NBS[City]=
	1 / SUM ( Maximum Feasible NBS[City, Neighbourhood!] )
	~	1/ha
	~	Indexing constant, derived as in v3: performance reads as a fraction of the \
		city's full feasible NBS potential.
	|

Unintended Social Ecological and Economic Consequences[City]=
	Total Functioning NBS[City] * Benefit per Hectare of NBS[City] * Maximum Harm Fraction
		 * ( 1 - NBS Implementation and Design Quality[City] )
		 + Residents Displaced by Green Gentrification[City] / SUM ( Population[City, Neighbourhood!] )
	~	Dmnl
	~	Aggregate adverse burden. First term, as in v3: harm from poorly delivered \
		NBS such as maladaptation, ecological disservices, public-space conflict \
		and cost overruns, scaling with how much exists and how badly it was done. \
		Second term, new: the share of the city's residents displaced by green \
		gentrification, weighted one for one with lost benefit.
	|

Realized NBS Performance[City]=
	MAX ( 0, Total Functioning NBS[City] * Benefit per Hectare of NBS[City]
		 - Unintended Social Ecological and Economic Consequences[City] )
	~	Dmnl
	~	Gross benefit net of the adverse burden, 0 to 1 of full feasible potential. \
		Drives support, the shortfall, and funding from other sectors.
	|

Desired NBS Performance[City]=
	Ambition Scale * Public and Political Support for NBS[City]
	~	Dmnl
	~	The performance the city is aiming for. Unchanged from v3.
	|

NBS Performance Shortfall[City]=
	MAX ( 0, Desired NBS Performance[City] - Realized NBS Performance[City] )
	~	Dmnl
	~	The single pressure signal driving both responses. Unchanged from v3.
	|

Rollout Pressure[City]=
	NBS Performance Shortfall[City] / Reference Shortfall
	~	Dmnl
	~	Urgency to deliver more now. Unchanged from v3.
	|

Need for Capability Building[City]=
	NBS Performance Shortfall[City] / ( NBS Performance Shortfall[City] + Reference Shortfall )
	~	Dmnl
	~	Recognised need to invest in skills and systems, 0 to 1, saturating while \
		Rollout Pressure is not. Unchanged from v3.
	|

Share of Resources to Rollout[City]=
	Maximum Rollout Share * XIDZ ( Rollout Pressure[City],
		 Rollout Pressure[City] + Pressure at Half Share, 0 )
	~	Dmnl
	~	The allocation decision between delivery and capability. Unchanged from v3.
	|

Capability Improvement Effort[City]=
	Resources for NBS Delivery[City] * ( 1 - Share of Resources to Rollout[City] )
		 * Need for Capability Building[City]
	~	kEUR/Year
	~	Resources actually spent on improvement. Unchanged from v3.
	|

Share of New NBS[Deprived]=
	Effective Share of New NBS in Deprived Areas ~~|
Share of New NBS[Other]=
	1 - Effective Share of New NBS in Deprived Areas
	~	Dmnl
	~	How the rollout budget is split between neighbourhood types. The arrow from \
		the targeting lever is positive for Deprived and negative for Other: it \
		divides a fixed budget.
	|

********************************************************
	.Resources and Cross Sectoral Integration
********************************************************~
		Municipal budget plus what other sectors contribute.
	|

Total NBS Resources[City]=
	Effective Municipal NBS Budget[City] + Funding from Other Sectors[City]
	~	kEUR/Year
	~	Budget, staff and managerial attention available for NBS, now the \
		municipal NBS budget plus funding from other sectors. A constant in v3.
	|

Funding from Other Sectors[City]=
	Effective Municipal NBS Budget[City] * Maximum Other Sector Funding Ratio
		 * Effective Cross Sectoral Integration * Realized NBS Performance[City]
	~	kEUR/Year
	~	Water, health, transport and housing budgets co-funding NBS. Other sectors \
		pay when two things hold: there is an institutional route for them to do so \
		(integration) and NBS visibly delivers their objectives (performance). This \
		closes a reinforcing loop: performance attracts funding, funding builds \
		more NBS, more NBS raises performance.
	|

Share of NBS Resources from Other Sectors[City]=
	XIDZ ( Funding from Other Sectors[City], Total NBS Resources[City], 0 )
	~	Dmnl
	~	INDICATOR, cross-sectoral policy integration. Share of all NBS resources \
		coming from sectors other than the NBS programme itself.
	|

Nature Programme Spending[City]=
	Total NBS Resources[City] * Effective Share of Resources to Nature Programmes
	~	kEUR/Year
	~	Resources spent on nature-based health programmes: green prescribing, guided \
		walks, physical-activity programmes in parks. New in v5.
	|

Resources for NBS Delivery[City]=
	Total NBS Resources[City] * ( 1 - Effective Share of Resources to Nature Programmes )
	~	kEUR/Year
	~	What is left to build NBS and build capability after programmes take their \
		share. Equal to Total NBS Resources when no programmes are funded, which is \
		why v5 reproduces v4 exactly by default. New in v5.
	|

Nature Programme Reach[City]=
	MIN ( 1, Nature Programme Spending[City]
		 / ( Programme Cost per Resident Reached * SUM ( Population[City, Neighbourhood!] ) ) )
	~	Dmnl
	~	Share of residents reached by nature programmes, 0 to 1. Spread evenly across \
		the city. New in v5.
	|

********************************************************
	.Indicators
********************************************************~
		Outcomes a city recognises, in the five domains of the proposal. Health
		and climate indicators use simple, published-style exposure-response
		relationships; every coefficient is a placeholder to be replaced with
		values for the pilot cities.
	|

Green Area[City, Neighbourhood]=
	Background Green Cover[City, Neighbourhood] * Land Area[City, Neighbourhood]
		 + Functioning NBS[City, Neighbourhood]
	~	ha
	~	All green cover in the neighbourhood: what exists independently of the NBS \
		programme (private gardens, woodland, natural areas) plus functioning \
		programme NBS.
	|

Green Cover[City, Neighbourhood]=
	Green Area[City, Neighbourhood] / Land Area[City, Neighbourhood]
	~	Dmnl
	~	INDICATOR, climate and ecosystem. Share of land under green cover, the \
		quantity behind the 30 percent canopy benchmark of the 3-30-300 rule.
	|

City Green Cover[City]=
	SUM ( Green Area[City, Neighbourhood!] ) / SUM ( Land Area[City, Neighbourhood!] )
	~	Dmnl
	~	INDICATOR, climate and ecosystem. Green cover across the whole city.
	|

Summer Cooling from Green Cover[City, Neighbourhood]=
	Summer Cooling per Unit Green Cover * Green Cover[City, Neighbourhood]
	~	degC
	~	INDICATOR, climate and ecosystem. How much cooler average summer air \
		temperature is than it would be with no green cover. Linear in cover, the \
		simplest form consistent with European heat studies.
	|

Premature Deaths Avoided by Green Cover[City, Neighbourhood]=
	Population[City, Neighbourhood] * Annual Death Rate[City]
		 * ( Heat Mortality Reduction per Degree * Summer Cooling from Green Cover[City, Neighbourhood]
		 + Mortality Reduction per Unit Green Cover * Green Cover[City, Neighbourhood] )
	~	person/Year
	~	INDICATOR, health. Deaths per year avoided compared with no green cover, \
		through two routes: cooler summers, and the wider benefits of living in \
		greener surroundings (physical activity, mental health, air quality). The \
		two routes overlap somewhat in the evidence, so read the sum as an upper \
		estimate.
	|

Green Cover Gap[City]=
	Green Cover[City, Other] - Green Cover[City, Deprived]
	~	Dmnl
	~	INDICATOR, social and environmental justice. How much greener the rest of \
		the city is than its deprived neighbourhoods, in cover share. Zero means \
		parity.
	|

Progress to Green Cover Target[City]=
	City Green Cover[City] / Green Cover Target
	~	Dmnl
	~	INDICATOR, transformative potential of existing policy. City green cover as \
		a fraction of the city's target: 1 means the target is met.
	|

Baseline Nature Engagement[Generic, Neighbourhood]=
	0.5, 0.6
	~	Dmnl [0,1]
	~	GENERIC CITY. CITY PROFILE. Share of residents with green space nearby who use it regularly \
		without any programme. Varies by culture: the proposal notes 66 percent of \
		Finnish adults exercise in parks against 20 percent of Maltese. New in v5. \
		SOURCE (v5.1): value kept. White et al. 2019, Scientific Reports 9:7730 \
		(England, n=19,806): 33.6 percent of adults spent 120 minutes or more in \
		nature in the past week. Among residents who have green space close by, \
		that is roughly one half; deprived groups visit less, hence 0.5 against 0.6. \
		Set per pilot from WP4 survey data.
	|

Background Fine Particulates[Generic, Neighbourhood]=
	10, 8
	~	ugm3 [0,100]
	~	GENERIC CITY. CITY PROFILE. Annual mean PM2.5 before new clean-air measures. New in v5. \
		SOURCE (v5.1): value kept. Proposal Table 4, from the ISGlobal ranking of \
		858 European cities: Paijat-Hame 5.8, Cork 7.2, Klagenfurt 13.4, Malta 17.5. \
		The WHO 2021 air quality guideline is 5. Deprived areas set higher because \
		they are more often near traffic and industry.
	|

Poor Mental Health Prevalence[Generic]=
	0.1
	~	Dmnl [0,0.5]
	~	GENERIC CITY. CITY PROFILE. Share of adults with poor mental health, here the common \
		mental disorders (depression and anxiety) that nature contact is linked to. \
		New in v5. SOURCE (v5.1): 0.15 -> 0.1. OECD/EC Health at a Glance: Europe \
		2018, from IHME estimates for 2016: anxiety disorders 5.4 percent and \
		depressive disorders 4.5 percent of the EU population; any mental health \
		problem, including substance use, 17.3 percent. Set per pilot from national \
		data or WP6.
	|

Expected NBS Lifetime[City]=
	Base NBS Lifetime / Decay Multiplier[City]
	~	Year
	~	INDICATOR, transformative potential of existing policy. How many years a 		hectare of NBS lasts under the city's current delivery quality. A policy 		that builds fast but short-lived NBS keeps the city on a replacement 		treadmill rather than transforming it.
	|

Green Space Access[City, Neighbourhood]=
	Green Cover[City, Neighbourhood] / ( Green Cover[City, Neighbourhood] + Green Cover at Half Access[City] )
	~	Dmnl
	~	Share of residents with usable green space close to home, the 300 in the \
		3-30-300 rule. Rises with green cover with diminishing returns. New in v5.
	|

Use of Green Space[City, Neighbourhood]=
	Green Space Access[City, Neighbourhood] * ( Baseline Nature Engagement[City, Neighbourhood]
		 + ( 1 - Baseline Nature Engagement[City, Neighbourhood] ) * Programme Effectiveness
		 * Nature Programme Reach[City] )
	~	Dmnl
	~	INDICATOR, health. Share of residents who regularly spend time in green space, \
		for example 120 minutes a week, including walking and cycling along greenways: \
		the nature dose WP4 measures. Needs access; programmes convert some of those \
		with access who would not otherwise go. New in v5.
	|

Poor Mental Health Cases Avoided[City, Neighbourhood]=
	Population[City, Neighbourhood] * Use of Green Space[City, Neighbourhood]
		 * Poor Mental Health Prevalence[City] * Mental Health Risk Reduction from Nature Use
	~	person
	~	INDICATOR, health. Residents who would otherwise have poor mental health, \
		the primary outcome of the WP6 health impact assessment. New in v5.
	|

Fine Particulate Concentration[City, Neighbourhood]=
	Background Fine Particulates[City, Neighbourhood]
		 * ( 1 - Effective Clean Air Policy Emission Cut )
		 * ( 1 - Particulate Removal per Unit Green Cover * Green Cover[City, Neighbourhood] )
	~	ugm3
	~	INDICATOR, climate and ecosystem. Annual mean PM2.5 in micrograms per cubic \
		metre (WHO guideline 5). Set mostly by emissions policy; green cover removes \
		only a little, which is itself worth showing decision makers. New in v5.
	|

Habitat Cover[City, Neighbourhood]=
	Background Green Cover[City, Neighbourhood] * Background Habitat Value
		 + Functioning NBS[City, Neighbourhood] / Land Area[City, Neighbourhood]
		 * ( Minimum NBS Habitat Value + ( 1 - Minimum NBS Habitat Value )
		 * NBS Implementation and Design Quality[City] )
	~	Dmnl
	~	INDICATOR, climate and ecosystem. Green cover weighted by its value as \
		habitat. Well-designed NBS (native, diverse, connected planting) count for \
		more, so municipal capability shows up in biodiversity. New in v5.
	|

********************************************************
	.City Profile Generic
********************************************************~
		Parameters that describe a particular city. Default values describe an
		illustrative mid-size European city of 150,000 people and are not data for
		any real city: replace them with each pilot city's values.
	|

Land Area[Generic, Neighbourhood]=
	600, 2400
	~	ha [10,100000]
	~	GENERIC CITY. CITY PROFILE. Land area of each neighbourhood type.
	|

Population[Generic, Neighbourhood]=
	45000, 105000
	~	person [100,10000000]
	~	GENERIC CITY. CITY PROFILE. Residents of each neighbourhood type.
	|

Background Green Cover[Generic, Neighbourhood]=
	0.06, 0.14
	~	Dmnl [0,0.9]
	~	GENERIC CITY. CITY PROFILE. Share of land under green cover that the NBS programme does \
		not manage and that is held constant: private gardens, woodland, natural \
		and agricultural land.
	|

Initial NBS Cover[Generic, Neighbourhood]=
	0.04, 0.08
	~	Dmnl [0,0.9]
	~	GENERIC CITY. CITY PROFILE. Share of land under programme NBS at the start of the run: \
		parks, street trees and other green infrastructure the city maintains.
	|

Maximum NBS Cover[Neighbourhood]=
	0.2, 0.25
	~	Dmnl [0.01,0.9]
	~	CITY PROFILE. The most land programme NBS could ever cover, given space, \
		buildings and competing uses. Low in dense historic cores.
	|

Municipal NBS Budget[Generic]=
	14400
	~	kEUR/Year [0,200000]
	~	GENERIC CITY. CITY PROFILE. Annual resources the city itself devotes to NBS, in thousand \
		euro, including staff time and managerial attention valued in money.
	|

Annual Death Rate[Generic]=
	0.01
	~	1/Year [0,0.03]
	~	GENERIC CITY. CITY PROFILE. Deaths per resident per year, all causes.
	|

Green Cover Target=
	0.3
	~	Dmnl [0.01,1]
	~	CITY PROFILE. The city's green cover target. 0.3 is the 30 percent of the \
		3-30-300 rule.
	|

********************************************************
	.Policy Levers
********************************************************~
		What a city decides. The first three are new in v4.
	|

Share of New NBS in Deprived Areas=
	0.2
	~	Dmnl [0,1]
	~	LEVER. Share of the rollout budget spent in deprived neighbourhoods. 0.2 is \
		roughly proportional to their land area in the default city.
	|

Displacement Safeguards=
	0.2
	~	Dmnl [0,1]
	~	LEVER. Strength of anti-displacement measures that accompany greening: rent \
		protection, social housing, community land trusts, just-green-enough \
		design. 0 none, 1 fully protective.
	|

Cross Sectoral Integration=
	0.2
	~	Dmnl [0,1]
	~	LEVER. Degree to which water, health, transport and housing departments \
		co-plan and co-fund NBS through shared targets and budgets. 0 none, 1 fully \
		integrated.
	|

Share of Resources to Nature Programmes=
	0
	~	Dmnl [0,0.5]
	~	LEVER. Share of NBS resources spent getting people into green space rather \
		than building or keeping it. 0 by default, which reproduces v4. New in v5.
	|

Clean Air Policy Emission Cut=
	0
	~	Dmnl [0,0.9]
	~	LEVER. Fractional cut in PM2.5 from clean-air and transport policy, outside \
		the NBS programme. New in v5.
	|

Maximum Rollout Share=
	0.7
	~	Dmnl [0,1]
	~	LEVER. Cap on the share of resources that can be diverted to immediate \
		rollout. Lower protects capability. From v3.
	|

Pressure at Half Share=
	0.35
	~	Dmnl [0.01,5]
	~	LEVER. Rollout pressure at which the city has swung half way to its cap. \
		From v3.
	|

Staff and Knowledge Loss=
	0.05
	~	1/Year [0,0.5]
	~	CONTEXT. Fractional annual loss of skills, staff and institutional memory. \
		From v3.
	|

Ambition Scale=
	0.9
	~	Dmnl [0,1]
	~	CONTEXT. Performance the city aims for when support is at its maximum. \
		From v3.
	|

********************************************************
	.Policy Timing
********************************************************~
		When a decision takes effect. New in v8.

		Before v8 a lever applied to the whole run, so changing one rewrote the city's
		past as well as its future. Here every lever is weighted by Policy Active: the
		city runs on its historical settings until Policy Start Year, then phases into
		the lever setting. The stretch of the run that will be calibrated against
		observed data is therefore fixed, whatever the levers say.

		Set Policy Start Year to 2011 (one phase-in before INITIAL TIME) to put every
		lever in force from the first time step, which is how v7 and earlier behaved,
		and which is how the regression tests reproduce v7 exactly.
	|

Policy Start Year=
	2027
	~	Year [2011,2075]
	~	LEVER. The year the policy settings take effect. 2027 by default: the first \
		year after the stretch of history the model will be calibrated against. New \
		in v8.
	|

Policy Phase In=
	1
	~	Year [0.25,10]
	~	How long a decision takes to reach full effect. A policy does not switch on \
		overnight, and a ramp also keeps the model free of a discontinuity. New in v8.
	|

Policy Active=
	MIN ( 1, MAX ( 0, ( Time - Policy Start Year ) / Policy Phase In ) )
	~	Dmnl
	~	0 before the policy start year, 1 once the decision has phased in. Every \
		lever is weighted by it. New in v8.
	|

Share of New NBS in Deprived Areas before Policy=
	0.2
	~	Dmnl [0,1]
	~	How the city targeted its greening historically. Equal to the lever's default, \
		so the default run is unchanged. New in v8.
	|

Displacement Safeguards before Policy=
	0.2
	~	Dmnl [0,1]
	~	The safeguards the city had in place historically. Equal to the lever's \
		default, so the default run is unchanged. New in v8.
	|

Cross Sectoral Integration before Policy=
	0.2
	~	Dmnl [0,1]
	~	How far the city's departments co-planned historically. Equal to the lever's \
		default, so the default run is unchanged. New in v8.
	|

Budget Change from Policy Start=
	1
	~	Dmnl [0,3]
	~	LEVER. What the city decides to do to its NBS budget from the policy start \
		year: 1 leaves it as it is, 1.5 raises it by half. A multiplier rather than a \
		second budget, so each city's calibrated budget stays the one place its \
		spending is recorded. New in v8.
	|

Effective Share of New NBS in Deprived Areas=
	Share of New NBS in Deprived Areas before Policy
		 + Policy Active * ( Share of New NBS in Deprived Areas
		 - Share of New NBS in Deprived Areas before Policy )
	~	Dmnl
	~	The targeting actually in force: the historical value until the policy start \
		year, the lever afterwards. New in v8.
	|

Effective Displacement Safeguards=
	Displacement Safeguards before Policy
		 + Policy Active * ( Displacement Safeguards - Displacement Safeguards before Policy )
	~	Dmnl
	~	The safeguards actually in force. New in v8.
	|

Effective Cross Sectoral Integration=
	Cross Sectoral Integration before Policy
		 + Policy Active * ( Cross Sectoral Integration - Cross Sectoral Integration before Policy )
	~	Dmnl
	~	The integration actually in force. New in v8.
	|

Effective Share of Resources to Nature Programmes=
	Policy Active * Share of Resources to Nature Programmes
	~	Dmnl
	~	The programme share actually in force. There is no historical value to carry: \
		whatever programmes a city already ran are inside its measured park use, so \
		the lever is the change from that, and it is 0 before the policy starts. New \
		in v8.
	|

Effective Clean Air Policy Emission Cut=
	Policy Active * Clean Air Policy Emission Cut
	~	Dmnl
	~	The clean-air cut actually in force. The lever is a cut relative to measured \
		PM2.5, which already contains whatever policy was in place, so it is 0 before \
		the policy starts. New in v8.
	|

Effective Municipal NBS Budget[City]=
	Municipal NBS Budget[City] * ( 1 + Policy Active * ( Budget Change from Policy Start - 1 ) )
	~	kEUR/Year
	~	The budget actually available: the city's calibrated budget until the policy \
		start year, then changed by the lever. New in v8.
	|

********************************************************
	.Evidence Parameters
********************************************************~
		Coefficients taken from the literature (v5.1). Each comment gives the \
		source, the derivation and the range the evidence supports. Two remain \
		assumptions, as the literature does not quantify them this way: \
		Displacement Sensitivity to Greening and Maximum Other Sector Funding Ratio. \
		Full references in NBS_Scaling_Quanti_v5.1_evidence.md.
	|

Summer Cooling per Unit Green Cover=
	2
	~	degC [0,10]
	~	Summer air-temperature reduction per unit of green cover share: 2 means \
		each 10 percentage points of cover cools by 0.2 degC. SOURCE (v5.1): 3 -> \
		2. Iungman et al. 2023, The Lancet 401:577-589 (93 European cities, summer \
		2015): raising tree cover from a population-weighted mean of 11 percent to \
		30 percent cools cities by 0.4 degC on average, about 2 degC per unit of \
		cover. Marando et al. 2022, Sustainable Cities and Society 77:103564 (601 \
		European cities): at least 16 percent tree cover is needed for 1 degC of \
		cooling, an upper bound near 6. Tree canopy cools more than grass, so NBS \
		mixes with much lawn would sit below 2. Range supported: 1 to 6.
	|

Heat Mortality Reduction per Degree=
	0.007
	~	1/degC [0,0.05]
	~	Fraction of annual deaths avoided per degree of summer cooling. SOURCE \
		(v5.1): value kept, now derived. Iungman et al. 2023, The Lancet: a \
		population-weighted urban heat island of 1.5 degC causes 4.33 percent \
		(95 percent CI 3.37 to 5.28) of summer (June to August) deaths, 2.9 \
		percent per degC. Summer is about 23 percent of annual deaths in the EU \
		(Eurostat monthly deaths 2019), so 0.029 x 0.23 = 0.007. Their 30 percent \
		tree-cover scenario (0.4 degC averts 1.84 percent of summer deaths) \
		implies 0.011, because cooling lands where it is hottest. Range 0.005 \
		to 0.011.
	|

Mortality Reduction per Unit Green Cover=
	0.2
	~	Dmnl [0,0.5]
	~	Fraction of annual deaths avoided per unit of green cover share: 0.2 means \
		10 percentage points of cover lowers mortality by 2 percent. SOURCE \
		(v5.1): value kept, now derived. Rojas-Rueda et al. 2019, Lancet \
		Planetary Health 3:e469-e477, meta-analysis of 9 cohorts, 8.3 million \
		people: hazard ratio 0.96 (0.94 to 0.97) per 0.1 NDVI within 500 m of \
		home. In the NDVI linear-mixture model (Gutman and Ignatov 1998), going \
		from sealed to fully vegetated land raises NDVI by about 0.5 to 0.8, so a \
		unit of green cover is 5 to 8 steps of 0.1 NDVI: 0.2 to 0.3. The lower \
		value is used because this route overlaps with the heat route (part of \
		the greenness association is cooling). Barboza et al. 2021, Lancet \
		Planetary Health, used the same function for 978 European cities. Range \
		0.12 to 0.32.
	|

Displacement Sensitivity to Greening=
	0.5
	~	Dmnl [0,3]
	~	Share of remaining residents displaced per unit share of the \
		neighbourhood's land greened, with no safeguards: 0.5 means greening 10 \
		percent of the land displaces 5 percent of residents. ASSUMPTION, checked \
		against the literature (v5.1): value kept. Green gentrification followed \
		greening in 17 of 28 European and North American cities (Anguelovski et al. \
		2022, Nature Communications 13:3816), but no study reports displacement \
		per hectare greened. In gentrifying neighbourhoods, vulnerable residents \
		leave at 0.4 to 0.5 percentage points a year above the usual rate (Ding, \
		Hwang and Divringi 2016, Regional Science and Urban Economics; Freeman \
		2005, Urban Affairs Review). A greening of 5 percent of a neighbourhood \
		followed by a decade of such excess moves displaces about 5 percent, a \
		sensitivity near 1, and about 0.6 once only 17 in 28 cities gentrify. \
		Range 0.2 to 1.5. Needs WP3 and pilot housing data.
	|

Maximum Other Sector Funding Ratio=
	1
	~	Dmnl [0,2]
	~	Funding from other sectors as a multiple of the municipal NBS budget, when \
		integration is complete and NBS delivers its full potential. ASSUMPTION, \
		checked against the literature (v5.1): value kept. About 75 percent of \
		European urban NBS are publicly funded, mostly by municipal budgets (Urban \
		Nature Atlas, 1000 NBS in 100 cities, Naturvation). Two anchors: the \
		English green social prescribing programme drew 0.47 of its funding from \
		local partners (1.66 million pounds against 3.5 million, national \
		evaluation 2024); the Copenhagen cloudburst plan is funded 75:25 by the \
		water utility and the city, a ratio of 3, in a flood-driven, legislated \
		case. Range 0.5 to 3.
	|

Programme Cost per Resident Reached=
	0.1
	~	kEUR/(person*Year) [0.001,1]
	~	Annual cost of one resident taking part in a nature programme, in thousand \
		euro: 0.1 is 100 euro. SOURCE (v5.1): 0.025 -> 0.1. Community health walks: \
		76 pounds per participant a year, national average (Natural England TIN055, \
		2009), about 85 euro then and 140 euro in today's prices; the cheapest \
		scheme reported, 25 pounds (Shropshire). Green social prescribing for \
		people with mental health needs: 507 pounds per participant, range 97 to \
		1481 (national evaluation, England, 2021-23), about 590 euro. The model's \
		programme is population-wide, so the walking-group value is used. Range \
		0.03 to 0.6.
	|

Programme Effectiveness=
	0.45
	~	Dmnl [0,1]
	~	Share of programme participants with access, who would not otherwise use \
		green space, who become regular users. SOURCE (v5.1): 0.4 -> 0.45. Pavey \
		et al. 2012, Journal of Epidemiology and Community Health, systematic review \
		of exercise referral schemes: pooled adherence 49 percent (40 to 59) in \
		observational studies and 43 percent (32 to 54) in trials. Adherence is \
		the right analogue because cost is per participant (uptake already paid \
		for). No nature-programme study reports sustained use this way; the \
		English green social prescribing evaluation found 85 percent uptake when \
		offered. Range 0.3 to 0.6.
	|

Green Cover at Half Access[Generic]=
	0.69
	~	Dmnl [0.01,1]
	~	GENERIC CITY. Green cover share at which half of residents have green space close to home \
		(the WHO recommendation used by ISGlobal: at least 0.5 ha within 300 m, the \
		300 of 3-30-300). SOURCE (v5.1): 0.2 -> 0.69, calibrated. Across European \
		cities 62 percent of residents lack this access (Barboza et al. 2021, \
		Lancet Planetary Health 5:e718-e730) while green and blue infrastructure \
		covers 42 percent of urban area on average (EEA 2022, Who benefits from \
		nature in cities?). Solving access = cover / ( cover + h ) gives h = 0.42 x \
		0.62 / 0.38 = 0.69. Recalibrate per pilot with h = cover x ( 1 - access ) / \
		access, using Table 4 access (Paijat-Hame 67.6, Cork 21.3, Klagenfurt 34.8, \
		Malta 23.8 percent) and the pilot's green cover.
	|

Mental Health Risk Reduction from Nature Use=
	0.25
	~	Dmnl [0,1]
	~	Fraction by which regular nature use lowers the risk of poor mental health. \
		SOURCE (v5.1): 0.2 -> 0.25. Shanahan et al. 2016, Scientific Reports \
		6:28551 (Brisbane, n=1538): odds ratio 1.37 (1.09 to 1.74) for depression \
		among people whose green space visits average under 30 minutes a week; \
		converted to a relative risk at the sample's prevalence, a 23 to 27 percent \
		lower risk for regular users. Cross-check: Liu et al. 2023, Environmental \
		Research 231:116303, meta-analysis: each 10 percent more green space lowers \
		the odds of depression by 3.7 percent (odds ratio 0.963, 0.948 to 0.979); \
		with these values the model gives about 1.2 percent through use alone, the \
		same direction and a conservative share. Range 0.08 to 0.4. Align with the \
		WP6 exposure-response once chosen.
	|

Particulate Removal per Unit Green Cover=
	0.004
	~	Dmnl [0,0.05]
	~	Fractional PM2.5 reduction per unit green cover share: 0.004 means 25 \
		percent cover removes 0.1 percent. SOURCE (v5.1): 0.05 -> 0.004. Nowak et \
		al. 2013, Environmental Pollution 178:395-402, ten US cities: trees improved \
		annual PM2.5 by 0.05 to 0.24 percent at tree cover of 16 to 52 percent, \
		0.002 to 0.007 per unit of cover, mean 0.004. Grass removes less than trees, \
		and in street canyons dense vegetation can trap traffic pollution and raise \
		local levels (Abhijith et al. 2017, Atmospheric Environment). Range 0 to \
		0.007.
	|

Background Habitat Value=
	0.3
	~	Dmnl [0,1]
	~	Habitat value of green cover outside the programme (gardens, woodland, farm \
		land), 0 to 1, where 1 is well-managed programme NBS. SOURCE (v5.1): 0.5 -> \
		0.3. England's statutory biodiversity metric (Defra 2024) scores habitat \
		as distinctiveness (low 2, medium 4, high 6) times condition (poor 1, \
		moderate 2, good 3); a medium habitat in good condition, the aim of good \
		urban greening, scores 12 per hectare and is set to 1 here. Vegetated \
		gardens and arable land are low distinctiveness (2/12 = 0.17); woodland is \
		high (1 or more). A mix of 60 percent gardens, 20 percent woodland and 20 \
		percent farm land gives about 0.3. Set per pilot from Urban Atlas land cover.
	|

Minimum NBS Habitat Value=
	0.17
	~	Dmnl [0,1]
	~	Habitat value of programme NBS delivered at zero quality (lawn, single \
		species); rises to 1 at perfect quality. SOURCE (v5.1): 0.2 -> 0.17. \
		Statutory biodiversity metric (Defra 2024): modified grassland, green roofs \
		and rain gardens are low distinctiveness; in poor condition they score 2 \
		per hectare against 12 for a medium habitat in good condition, 2/12 = 0.17.
	|

********************************************************
	.Delivery Parameters
********************************************************~
		From v3. Resource quantities are rescaled to thousand euro and hectares so
		that, for a homogeneous city, v4 reproduces v3 exactly.
	|

Initial Municipal NBS Capability=
	0.45
	~	Dmnl [0,1]
	~	Municipal capability at the start of the run, as a fraction of best \
		practice.
	|

Initial Public and Political Support=
	0.5
	~	Dmnl [0,1]
	~	Public and political support at the start of the run.
	|

Delivery Cost per Hectare[Generic]=
	200
	~	kEUR/ha [10,5000]
	~	GENERIC CITY. Resources needed to deliver one hectare of functioning green cover at \
		perfect quality, in thousand euro.
	|

Minimum Delivery Time=
	1
	~	Year [1,10]
	~	Shortest time in which a neighbourhood's remaining room for NBS could be \
		filled, however much money is available: design, procurement, planting \
		and establishment all take time. It also bounds the model's fastest rate, \
		so that any city's parameter values integrate accurately.
	|

Base NBS Lifetime=
	50
	~	Year [5,200]
	~	Life of functioning NBS at perfect quality.
	|

Extra Decay at Zero Quality=
	3
	~	Dmnl [0,9]
	~	Additional multiples of the baseline decay rate when delivery quality is \
		zero.
	|

Minimum Quality=
	0.2
	~	Dmnl [0,1]
	~	Quality floor. Even a city with no capability delivers something.
	|

Capability Build Time=
	6
	~	Year [2,50]
	~	Time to close the capability gap at full investment.
	|

Maximum Improvement Absorption=
	2
	~	Dmnl [1,5]
	~	Ceiling on how fast capability building can run relative to its reference \
		rate, however much money is spent.
	|

Reference Capability Investment[City]=
	Reference Capability Investment Share * Effective Municipal NBS Budget[City]
	~	kEUR/Year
	~	Improvement spend that supports capability building at full rate. New in v6: \
		a fixed share of the city's budget, so that results do not depend on city \
		size. In v5.1 it was a fixed 8,100 thousand euro, which let a larger city \
		build capability faster only because it was larger: a Malta-sized copy of \
		the generic city reached capability 0.79 instead of 0.55 (feasibility test, \
		2026-09-19).
	|

Reference Capability Investment Share=
	0.5625
	~	Dmnl [0.01,10]
	~	Reference capability investment as a share of the municipal NBS budget. \
		0.5625 is v5.1's 8,100 over the generic budget of 14,400, so the generic \
		city is unchanged. New in v6.
	|


Support Adjustment Time=
	4
	~	Year [2,50]
	~	Time constant for support to adjust. Also carries the perception lag.
	|

Harm Sensitivity=
	3
	~	Dmnl [0,3]
	~	How strongly adverse effects erode support.
	|

Baseline Support Decay=
	0.15
	~	Dmnl [0,1]
	~	Fade in political attention absent any visible delivery.
	|

Reference Shortfall=
	0.15
	~	Dmnl [0.01,1]
	~	Shortfall that counts as normal pressure.
	|

Maximum Harm Fraction=
	0.5
	~	Dmnl [0,1]
	~	Harm from poor delivery as a share of gross benefit at zero quality.
	|

